7
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:53,000
 Three technologies: Mini Filter Drivers, Window Filtering Platform, and EX version, Kernel Notification Routines.

8
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:59,000
 Mini Filter Drivers are essentially a hook or callback for I/O requests, okay?

9
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:06,000
 So when you're trying to write or read from a file, you can create a hook with those and approve or deny based off of the Threat Locker ACL.

10
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:11,000
 Windows Filtering Platform, similar situation, but for Connect Bind requests, right?

11
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:16,000
 You can say, okay, hey, is this process allowed to talk to Port 443 over there on the internet?

12
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:17,000
 Yes or no?

13
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:18,000
 Approve or deny.

14
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:21,000
 Lastly, you've got EX version kernel notification routines.

15
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:27,000
 The EX version is important because it allows you to approve or deny versus just getting a notification from the kernel.

16
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:37,000
 And this is specific for hooking various native API calls like ntCreateUserProcess, which is what you would use to spin up PowerShell.exe or something like that, okay?

17
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:42,000
 So those three are very useful to Threat Locker's ring fencing core technology.

