60
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:32,000
 But the OAuth 2.1 standard states that PKC is mandatory.

61
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:35,000
 The implicit flow is being removed.

62
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:37,000
 The client credentials...

63
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:39,000
 Sorry, not client credentials.

64
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:42,000
 The password credentials flow is getting removed.

65
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:48,000
 Redirect URI matching has to be exact string only instead of any wildcards

66
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:55,000
 or some of the loose path matching that it does now in some frameworks.

67
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:00,000
 Bearer tokens are no longer allowed to be in URIs either.

68
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:05,000
 So that massively changes the attack surface from what we're used to.

69
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:12,000
 So just to summarize, servers must reject requests that miss the code challenge parameter.

70
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:17,000
 Implicit and parser credentials flows are going to die out.

71
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:20,000
 Redirect URIs require exact string matching.

72
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:25,000
 No more access tokens in URIs.

73
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:32,000
 And this is quite interesting because it brings quite a significant change to what we're used to.

74
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:37,000
 So the new proposed changes, which some frameworks are already implementing,

75
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:45,000
 is that the authorization header is preferred for transmitting the token instead of the URI.

76
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:49,000
 And also if this header can't be utilized for whatever reason,

77
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:54,000
 it can be transmitted as formally coded within a request body.

78
00:05:54,000 --> 00:06:02,000
 So what led me to this is one of the targets, I can't say, unfortunately,

