86
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:40,000
 Now, the MCP authorization spec.

87
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:43,000
 I've got a diagram up for people that aren't listening.

88
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:46,000
 Builds on top of OAuth 2.1.

89
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:52,000
 That then uses and builds on top of RFC 8414 and RFC 7591,

90
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:58,000
 which is server metadata discovery and dynamic client registration.

91
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:04,000
 And that then builds the MCP authorization spec as a whole.

92
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:15,000
 So the traditional flow that we're used to with the dynamic client registration flow is we send a HTTP post request to the register endpoint,

93
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:25,000
 which contains redirect URI, grant type, logo, client name, and then the authorization server returns client ID, client secret, and issue that.

94
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:34,000
 Now, we're very much used to that traditional threat model when you introduce the logo URIs for SSRF and things like that.

95
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:43,000
 But now with the new introduced client identity metadata document, that changes a little bit.

96
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:57,000
 So the flow here is the agent sends a request to the authorization server containing a client manifest URI, a scope, and a response type.

97
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:06,000
 That authorization server takes out and stores it in the metadata verification service or verifies it there as well.

98
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:15,000
 And it's parsing and validating the redirect URI, the JWKS URI, logo URI, and so on.

99
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:21,000
 And then that metadata verification service sends that back to the authorization server.

100
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:31,000
 Yes, no, whatever, provides a consent and login screen, and then parses that auth code back to the AI agent.

101
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:43,000
 Now, this is interesting because the threat model changes here a little bit because, I mean, the authorization server and the metadata verification service can be on the same host.

102
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:52,000
 But we're now parsing a client manifest URI to both of these services where it has to be validated.

103
00:08:52,000 --> 00:09:01,000
 So the most logical thing to go after when we're looking at this new implementation is SSRF.

104
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:17,000
 Now, SSRF on the metadata verification service is very much still going to be present because there's either going to be the authorization server itself handles that or the external identity verification service handles that.

105
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:25,000
 And by the very nature of it, they have to request the client manifest URI and validate that themselves.

106
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:33,000
 So when we start looking at how that validation occurs, which is some of the things I was doing on one of these targets in the live event,

107
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:41,000
 it's trying to understand, okay, when this flow happens, first of all, understand what is the user agent that's making this flow?

108
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:55,000
 Second of all, off the back of that, does the service that's handling the redirect and actually handling the request of that document, does it handle different response codes?

109
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:57,000
 How does it handle those different response codes?

110
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:00,000
 Does it handle different 30X response codes differently?

111
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:07,000
 Are there any specific quirks on that specific user agent if we have access to that?

112
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:11,000
 A long shot, but does it handle JavaScript processing as well?

113
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:14,000
 If it's maybe a headless browser that's doing that?

114
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:18,000
 And going down those very traditional SSRF attack vectors.

115
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:28,000
 And it's quite nice because there's two different spots where this might potentially occur as well with this new client identity metadata document registration,

116
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:34,000
 because you have the first request to the client manifest and then inside the client manifest as well,

117
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:40,000
 you can nest a whole range of URIs as well, which may or may not be supported depending on the framework.

118
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:47,000
 So there's two different places where this can really come up and increase that attack surface.

119
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:55,000
 Now, the other thing as well with this is because the client must fetch the client JSON document

120
00:10:55,000 --> 00:11:02,000
 and must enforce that or redirect URIs and the document belong to the same domain.

121
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:07,000
 We start to get into the standard, how is the domain validation made?

122
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:20,000
 So, say for example, if the service isn't validating that the domain you pass in as a redirect URI doesn't match your actual own domain,

123
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:28,000
 you might be able to set a redirect, for example, to attacker.com or victim.com/callback.

124
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:36,000
 It might accept that weak domain check and you might be able to use that as a primitive to leak to an external domain as well.

125
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:47,000
 And the other thing that I just wanted to touch upon as well that I did touch upon is the URIs in the CI/MD flow,

126
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:49,000
 which you can pass in inside the client.json.

127
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:52,000
 I just wanted to clarify this.

128
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:55,000
 It's the logo URI and the JWKS URI.

129
00:11:55,000 --> 00:12:07,000
 So, test case in your client.json, you nest the logo URI as a target, say for example, internal, whatever it might be,

130
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:09,000
 metadata or endpoint or internal host.

131
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:11,000
 You set that in there.

132
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:23,000
 So, if the tool server fetches the logo URI blindly and maybe displays the logo in the UI or gives an error on that, then you've got SSRF there as well.

133
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:32,000
 And the other thing as well which I was thinking about when I started to threat model this service is since CI/MD is a static document,

134
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:38,000
 the authorization server might start to cache some of these requests.

135
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:50,000
 So, for example, if it's validating the client.json and it caches the results, that gives a whole leeway there for your traditional cache deception

136
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:53,000
 and all those types of attack vectors as well.

137
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:59,000
 So, overall, I'm just going to share the client identity metadata document again.

138
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:10,000
 So, overall, even though the dynamic registration is going to be slowly phased out over time,

139
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:16,000
 if this becomes a standard, which I think it will be and slowly is,

140
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:22,000
 it actually opens up some more attack surface because we have a little bit more to play with in this flow as well

