 So, that's the second change I thought that was quite interesting when I started to deep dive and look at some of these documents off the back of the target.

143
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:38,000
 Okay, so, on to the next one and that is the token exchange RFC 8693.

144
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:47,000
 So, right now, agent authentication is in a good spot, but agent authorization not so much.

145
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:49,000
 I'm just going to show my screen.

146
00:13:49,000 --> 00:14:02,000
 Now, right now, there's a lot of token pass-throughs everywhere, which is if you hack AI or if you're looking at these multi-agent workflows on the blue team side,

147
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:06,000
 you'll know that this is a problem where token pass-through right now.

148
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:11,000
 So, the user token has all the scopes which then get past agent A.

149
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:21,000
 Agent A needs to spin up agent B and agent C in an agentic workflow and delegate all these different tasks over.

150
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:25,000
 And right now, all these agents inherit the same token and the same scope.

151
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:38,000
 Now, the token exchange RFC 8693 gives a mean for agent to agent communication to reduce the scope on each hop.

152
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:42,000
 So, say for example, your user token, the scope is full as you'd expect.

153
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:45,000
 You give that to agent A.

154
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:50,000
 And the scope you only want to give agent A because it handles a very specific task.

155
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:53,000
 It's just a calendar read, for example.

156
00:14:53,000 --> 00:15:04,000
 Now, when agent A invokes agent B, for example, it can do that on the next hop and just give it a reduced scope for that hop.

157
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:14,000
 So, you get from this situation where you have a full scopes token being passed from every single agent and it just gets passed downstream.

158
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:25,000
 If you look at any of this traffic, it will simply just have a authorization header with that token and each agent just appends it on and tags it on and sends it to the next call.

159
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:38,000
 Now, this addresses that where you can reduce the scope for each hop in that agent workflow on agent to agent communications.

160
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:40,000
 So, that's pretty nice.

161
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:48,000
 And to be honest, if you're in any AI circle, that's been spoken about quite a while now where authorization is just a mess.

162
00:15:48,000 --> 00:16:03,000
 If you look at any targets, I think one of the most common bugs is around confused deputy attacks and just agentic services or chatbots, for example, just having almost God mode to everything.

163
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:15,000
 So, this, although slightly different, aims to address some of that over-privileged scope creep that keeps cropping up all over the show.

164
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:18,000
 So, that would be quite nice when it's implemented.

165
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:22,000
 Well, not nice for us, but something to look out for at least.

166
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:31,000
 Okay, some other things before moving on, I just wanted to mention on the token exchange as well, is that this is already cropping up in a few targets.

167
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:44,000
 The one that I'm aware of is Auth0, the token vault utilizes this architecture for enabling the AI agent integrations.

168
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:57,000
 And the other thing as well, token pass-through, when you directly forward user credentials, is a primary driver for confused deputy exploits.

169
00:16:57,000 --> 00:17:11,000
 Now, I know in one of the live events that people were just in Japan, there were a lot of these confused deputy-based attack vectors happening on this target.

170
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:14,000
 So, definitely something to keep an eye out for.

171
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:24,000
 Okay, so, in OAuth 2.0, PKCE is now required by default instead of being optional.

172
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:31,000
 And PKCE, proof key for code exchange, is used to prevent authorization code interception, essentially.

173
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:37,000
 And you would have probably seen it on requests through the code challenge parameter.

174
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:52,000
 Now, it's interesting because in a normal PKCE flow, the client will send its code challenge, the server stores that code challenge,

175
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:59,000
 the client then sends a code verifier, and then that's then verified, and if they match, you get the token issued.

176
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:07,000
 So, with a downgrade attack, the code challenge is stripped from the request or set to a default value.

177
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:19,000
 If the server accepts the request without it, that then facilitates the authorization codes to be stolen and then exchanged without no verifier needed.

178
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:31,000
 So, the main vector here, when this is implemented by default on frameworks that are 2.1 compliant or are migrating to be 2.1 compliant,

179
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:34,000
 is looking around the PKCE downgrade attacks.

180
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:42,000
 Now, I do have a couple of CBEs that I found off the back of this research as well, which highlight this quite nicely.

181
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:48,000
 So, one of the big ones that I saw, which went under my radar initially, was by Zeropath.

182
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:54,000
 And essentially, it was in Django Olof, which is quite a big library.

183
00:18:54,000 --> 00:19:05,000
 And the implementation flaw was in the fact that it utilized the preferred username from the Olof response as the account UID.

184
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:17,000
 So, when you are looking at these frameworks, it's important to understand that what claims should be writable and what claims should be used as your source of truth.

185
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:22,000
 Because there are claims that are mutable or writable by users themselves.

186
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:29,000
 And in this case, preferred username was actually a mutable claim on certain providers.

187
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:36,000
 So, what this allows to do is an attacker, for example, can set their preferred username themselves.

188
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:41,000
 They just set that to admin or victim.com, and they can get account takeover on that.

189
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:46,000
 I'm just going to show my screen on this research, because it does deserve a shout out.

190
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:58,000
 Now, as I said, by Zeropath, and it was seven vulnerabilities in Django Olof, which let them achieve account impersonation.

191
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:04,000
 Now, it is a pretty big library, as I said, with 2 million monthly downloads.

192
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:18,000
 And four major vulnerabilities are identified in the library with the different providers that it integrates with is that Okta identifiers were mutable.

193
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:21,000
 NetIQ identifiers were also mutable.

194
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:25,000
 Tokens for deactivated users could be refreshed indefinitely.

195
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:36,000
 Now, in these live events, the reason why I wanted to share this research is because these are the exact things I was looking for in some of these implementations as well.

196
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:52,000
 And that is, if a user gets deactivated or if the state of that user account gets changed in some way to a state where the user shouldn't be able to access something, how is that user's token handled?

197
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:56,000
 And how is the user's refresh token handled as well?

198
00:20:56,000 --> 00:21:05,000
 Can you just use the refresh token to still get the same claims and same attributes as it was when it was first issued?

199
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:08,000
 Or is the refresh token also updated on the server side as well?

200
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:15,000
 Now, the biggest vulnerabilities from this right up are obviously the mutable attributes being used for certain providers.

201
00:21:15,000 --> 00:21:23,000
 So as it says here, the function uses the OAuth response field preferred username as the account UID.

202
00:21:23,000 --> 00:21:32,000
 Many OAuth and OIDC providers expose a stable sub subject claim and an optimal user facing field like preferred username.

203
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:36,000
 Preferred username is often mutable and not globally unique.

204
00:21:36,000 --> 00:21:56,000
 Now that's important here because when an application or a library is identifying its users for a claim, that claim has to be immutable because otherwise a user can just create their own claim or overwrite an existing claim and use it as a source of truth.

205
00:21:56,000 --> 00:22:02,000
 Now, this is actually also can be quite an interesting primitive for privilege escalation bugs.

206
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:21,000
 So if you see a permission set or something else as a claim which one you can overwrite and two is being used as a source of truth for something in the application, definitely try and overwrite that because it can lead to some really interesting bugs.

207
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:25,000
 So just keep track of that as well.

208
00:22:25,000 --> 00:22:34,000
 I think this blog writer was a runescape player as well if you get the Zezima reference, however you say it.

209
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:37,000
 But yeah, quite a good blog post.

210
00:22:37,000 --> 00:22:39,000
 It contains code snippets as well.

211
00:22:39,000 --> 00:22:42,000
 So definitely go and check that one out by zero point auth.

212
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:45,000
 But those are the two points I wanted to mention.

213
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:58,000
 Now, the other one I wanted to mention is touches upon the point that we just talked about around the new RFCs and where some of this ambiguity is going to start coming in on implementation floors.

214
00:22:58,000 --> 00:23:04,000
 And that one is a PKCE bypass on Cloudflare workers.

215
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:11,000
 Now, this one took a little bit of digging and I've got the GitHub advisory shared on screen right now.

216
00:23:11,000 --> 00:23:15,000
 But essentially the bug was in the handle token request.

217
00:23:15,000 --> 00:23:22,000
 It accepted a code verify even if the initial authorization request omitted the code challenge.

218
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:32,000
 So by stripping the code challenge, you could essentially exploit the lack of enforcement and steal the auth code and exchange it without a valid verifier.

219
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:36,000
 And now this one affected all Cloudflare workers as well.

220
00:23:36,000 --> 00:23:43,000
 And you can see here that the MCP specification requires IOF 2.0 stated in that advisory as well.

221
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:52,000
 So yeah, quite a interesting one off the back of some of the new implementation.

222
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:53,000
 Okay.

223
00:23:53,000 --> 00:24:05,000
 So the next one is again by Xeropath and it is, let me share my screen, CVE-2025-5476.

224
00:24:05,000 --> 00:24:18,000
 And it is a authentication bypass on IOF 2.0 proxy, which is quite a big and popular gateway use for Kubernetes and cloud environments.

225
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:28,000
 And essentially there's a feature in this framework that allows you to specify regex patterns for paths that should bypass authentication completely.

226
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:39,000
 So say if you have a specific path that you needed to be publicly accessible and you didn't want it wrapped or sat behind the proxy, you would just go, okay, here's a regex for that path and have at it.

227
00:24:39,000 --> 00:24:41,000
 And it would be publicly accessible.

228
00:24:41,000 --> 00:24:49,000
 Now, there's an implementation flaw in the fact that the regex actually matched against the entire request URI.

229
00:24:49,000 --> 00:24:56,000
 Now that's interesting because if you match against the entire request URI, you have query parameters in there as well.

230
00:24:56,000 --> 00:25:15,000
 And that meant that you could essentially match a request by doing something like /endpoint/n.0 access/parameter= and then the regex, which is used to skip that path.

231
00:25:15,000 --> 00:25:25,000
 And because the parameter is also matched from that regex, it would lead to a complete auth bypass for those specific endpoints.

232
00:25:25,000 --> 00:25:43,000
 And what that would allow an attacker to do is actually get authentication being skipped for requests and endpoints that should have been protected simply by matching that bypass regex as a request parameter in the URI instead of actually being the URI itself.

233
00:25:43,000 --> 00:25:47,000
 So quite a, quite an interesting CVE.

234
00:25:47,000 --> 00:25:55,000
 But yeah, this was by zero point CVE 2025 54576 on OAuth 2 proxy.

235
00:25:55,000 --> 00:26:07,000
 Now, the last one that did make me laugh, which I'll share shortly, is from Pentester Labs, CVE from this year, quite fresh.

236
00:26:07,000 --> 00:26:11,000
 And I didn't know that HARBOR existed as a language.

237
00:26:11,000 --> 00:26:17,000
 I didn't even know what HARBOR was until I found this out, but still the bug's pretty funny.

238
00:26:19,000 --> 00:26:27,000
 But anyway, the TLDR here is that the classic none algorithm is supported.

239
00:26:27,000 --> 00:26:33,000
 And in this one, it was actually any unknown algorithm gets that bypass.

240
00:26:33,000 --> 00:26:41,000
 So quite funny as you can set the algorithm to Badana or ZZZ or whatever it might be.

241
00:26:41,000 --> 00:26:45,000
 It gets passed to the framework or library or you pass in none.

242
00:26:45,000 --> 00:26:51,000
 And it just basically fails open and bypasses signature verification altogether.

243
00:26:51,000 --> 00:26:57,000
 So that was CVE 202623993 by Pentester Labs.

244
00:26:57,000 --> 00:26:59,000
 So check that one out as well.

245
00:26:59,000 --> 00:27:00,000
 Okay.

246
00:27:00,000 --> 00:27:11,000
 So just to wrap up for everyone, a bit of a shorter episode today, but just cover some really cool changes and interesting things that's going to start creeping in to things we're looking at.

247
00:27:11,000 --> 00:27:16,000
 So the core OAuth 2.1 protocol PKC is mandatory.

248
00:27:16,000 --> 00:27:20,000
 Servers must reject requests that don't contain the code challenge.

249
00:27:20,000 --> 00:27:24,000
 Implicit flow and parser credentials are dead.

250
00:27:24,000 --> 00:27:25,000
 They're going to be phased out.

251
00:27:25,000 --> 00:27:33,000
 Redirect URIs now require exact string matching instead of wildcard matching that sometimes we're used to.

252
00:27:33,000 --> 00:27:36,000
 Bearer tokens are prohibited in URIs.

253
00:27:36,000 --> 00:27:43,000
 PKC downgrade attacks are going to be quite fruitful.

254
00:27:44,000 --> 00:28:01,000
 And some other things as well around where some of these bugs might come up is look for the transition gaps when frameworks are going from 2.0 to 2.1 and trying to be cross compatible and make sure all their clients are still in a working state.

255
00:28:01,000 --> 00:28:09,000
 Is that there's going to be a lot of compatibility shims where a lot of bugs show up in.

256
00:28:09,000 --> 00:28:16,000
 So if you see a target claiming OAuth 2.1 compliance, check out the downgrade attacks for examples.

257
00:28:16,000 --> 00:28:28,000
 Now the second takeaway is the CIMD SSRF for the new way for the client metadata to register.

258
00:28:28,000 --> 00:28:31,000
 Now that's an SSRF goldmine.

259
00:28:31,000 --> 00:28:44,000
 So if you see that implemented or see support for it, definitely check it out and pass in that metadata document and nest that with your own redirect URIs or targets, whatever you want to do as well.

260
00:28:46,000 --> 00:28:58,000
 Now the third point is authentication is in a fairly strong spot, but delegation and authorization is still in a pretty bad spot and there's a lot of bugs there.

261
00:28:58,000 --> 00:29:00,000
 So look for token pass through.

262
00:29:00,000 --> 00:29:07,000
 If an agent is calling at all, try and disclose how it calls that tool.

263
00:29:07,000 --> 00:29:22,000
 Look at the traffic if you can understand the request structure and see if the tool includes a full privilege token rather than a small scopes token just for that specific action.

264
00:29:22,000 --> 00:29:25,000
 So that's the takeaways for this week, guys.

265
00:29:25,000 --> 00:29:40,000
 I think OAuth 2.1 is going to be quite interesting when we start seeing a lot of the frameworks and libraries that we interact with start implementing it and trying to get a lot of these changes cross compatible and ensuring that clients are supported.

